      "math_academy_id": "q-117093",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ and ${f}^{″}(x)$ are undefined at $x=-2$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$ then ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and if $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{″}(x)<0$",
      "worked_solution": "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘\\,\\,⌣$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗\\,\\,⌢$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=-2$, where the derivative is undefined.\n\n- Since $x=-2$ is a local minimum, the graph must go up to the left and to the right of that point.\n\n- The graph must be concave  up  on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  concave down  on $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193742/assets/35e5a713c8649bee4fb1a03f44a87feef082d9046168d8d0486b161c530327dc.png)",
